+\begin{abstract}
+Generated prose can exhibit a recognizable machine-like register, but a
+surface regularity, a hidden representation, and a controllable behavior are
+different empirical claims.  We connect them in a staged test built from two
+matched corpora of 500 English continuation pairs.  Thirty-five frozen
+linguistic measurements define lexical and syntactic realization directions;
+cross-generator readouts map those directions into model states; and a
+source-consistent Jacobian pullback tests fixed-token transport before a
+separate generation experiment tests realization.  In Llama~3.1~8B and
+Qwen3~4B, the lexical transport curve exceeds all 100 complete matched-null
+pipelines, whereas the syntactic curve does not.  Neither direction yields
+reliable component-selective control in free generation, and a one-seed Qwen
+syntactic inversion changes sign across three new seeds.  The result locates
+a boundary in the causal story: a linguistic signature can have a
+reproducible hidden trace without becoming a portable behavioral control.
+\end{abstract}
